/// Shadowing is per FILE, and this is the only program that can show it.
///
/// ⚠️ Every other test of shadowing puts the declaration and the use of the
/// shadowed name in ONE file, which is the case the rule gets right by
/// accident. Three files are the smallest arrangement that tells the per-file
/// rule apart from a program-wide one:
///
///   - this file declares 'Length' and imports both units;
///   - lib/Loud EXPORTS a 'Str', so this file inherits that shadow;
///   - lib/Quiet imports nothing, so every name in it is the built-in.
///
/// A program-wide shadow set passes lines 1 and 3 and fails 2 and 5 -- and
/// fails them at 'cc' rather than in the output, because the C calls a static
/// belonging to another translation unit.

uses 'lib/Loud';
uses 'lib/Quiet';

/// Declared here, at the top level of the root file.
function Length (X) : String;
begin
    Exit 'root shadow';
end

begin
    // 1. The file's own declaration wins in the file that made it.
    WriteLn (Length ('abcd'));

    // 2. A unit this file imports does NOT see that declaration.
    WriteLn (Quiet.Measure ('abcd'));
    WriteLn (Measure ('abcd'));

    // 3. A shadow a unit EXPORTS does reach the file that imported it.
    WriteLn (Str (1));

    // 4. ...and is what the exporting unit sees itself.
    WriteLn (Loud.Own());

    // 5. But it does not reach a unit that never imported it.
    WriteLn (Quiet.Plain());

    // 6. System reaches past every one of them.
    WriteLn (System.Length ('abcd'));
    WriteLn (System.Str (1));
end
